  1185	\section{Pair gates and the complete post-establishment path}
  1186	
  1187	For the center, let
  1188	\[
  1189	 I_H=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}\frac1{d_x}
  1190	 =1+\frac1{C-1+m},
  1191	\qquad
  1192	 J_H(r)=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}
  1193	 \frac{h_H^{\dB}(\{x\};r)}{d_x},
  1194	\]
  1195	where $h_H^{\dB}$ is the isolated-center dB fixation committor.
  1196	For a pair with internal weight $W=C/\sigma$, put
  1197	\[
  1198	 I_P=\frac2W=\frac{2\sigma}{C},\qquad
  1199	 J_P(r)=\frac1W=\frac{\sigma}{C}.
  1200	\]
  1201	The second identity uses the exact dB singleton value $1/2$ on $K_2$.
  1202	The center estimates imply
  1203	\[
  1204	 I_H=1+o(1),\qquad J_H(r)=p+o(1),
  1205	\]
  1206	while $J_H(1/r)$ and the reciprocal core fixation probability are
  1207	$o(C^{-1})$.
  1208	
  1209	\begin{proposition}[Gate odds and global sweep]
  1210	\label{prop:gate}
  1211	For every fixed $r>1$, the separated macro-fixation probabilities satisfy
  1212	\[
  1213	 P_U^H=1-o(q/C),
  1214	 \qquad
  1215	 P_{\Bd}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_B}{1+Z_B},
  1216	 \qquad
  1217	 P_{\dB}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_D}{1+Z_D},
  1218	\]
  1219	where
  1220	\[
  1221	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1222	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1223	\]
  1224	The convergence is uniform when $r$ ranges over a compact subinterval of
  1225	$(1,\infty)$.
  1226	\end{proposition}
  1227	
  1228	\begin{proof}
  1229	For one specified pair, let $A,D,B,C'$ denote, respectively, mutant-pair
  1230	invasion of a resident center, resident-center recovery of that pair,
  1231	mutant-center invasion of a resident pair, and resident-pair recovery of
  1232	the center.  In each row below $a_P$ means the isolated pair fixation
  1233	$a_P^U$ for that update rule.  Summing the parent--target or death--parent events gives,
  1234	after a common time change,
  1235	\[
  1236	\begin{array}{c|cccc}
  1237	 &A&D&B&C'\\ \hline
  1238	 \Bd&
  1239	 CrI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(r)&
  1240	 2I_Ha_P(1/r)&
  1241	 2rI_Ha_P(r)&
  1242	 CI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(1/r)\\[1mm]
  1243	 \dB&
  1244	 2rJ_H(r)&
  1245	 (C/r)J_P(1/r)&
  1246	 CrJ_P(r)&
  1247	 (2/r)J_H(1/r).
  1248	\end{array}
  1249	\]
  1250	The isolated pair values are
  1251	\[
  1252	 a_P^{\Bd}(r)=\frac r{r+1},\quad
  1253	 a_P^{\Bd}(1/r)=\frac1{r+1},\quad
  1254	 a_P^{\dB}(r)=a_P^{\dB}(1/r)=\frac12.
  1255	\]
  1256	
  1257	On macro states the only nonzero rates are
  1258	\[
  1259	\begin{array}{ll}
  1260	 (0,k)\to(1,k):kA,&(0,k)\to(0,k-1):kD,\\
  1261	 (1,k)\to(1,k+1):(q-k)B,&(1,k)\to(0,k):(q-k)C'.
  1262	\end{array}
  1263	\]
  1264	From $(1,k)$, the chance that the center reverses before the next pair
  1265	conversion is $C'/(B+C')$.  A union bound over at most $q$ conversions gives
  1266	an upper bound on failure, because failure requires at least one such
  1267	reversal even though a reversal need not imply failure:
  1268	\[
  1269	 1-P_U^H\le q\frac{C'}{B+C'}.
  1270	\]
  1271	Proposition~\ref{prop:center} and the portal estimates give
  1272	$qC'/B=o(q/C)$, uniformly on each fitness compact.  Thus a mutant center
  1273	converts all resident pairs with probability $1-o(q/C)$.
  1274	
  1275	From $(0,1)$, the first successful macro event is either center invasion at
  1276	rate $A$ or loss of the sole mutant pair at rate $D$.  Hence
  1277	\[
  1278	 P_U^P=\frac{A}{A+D}\,p_{1,1},
  1279	\]
  1280	where $p_{1,1}$ is the macro-fixation probability from $(1,1)$.  The same
  1281	sweep bound, now with at most $q-1$ remaining conversions, gives
  1282	$p_{1,1}\to1$.  (Equivalently,
  1283	$P_U^P=[A/(A+D)][(B+C')/B]P_U^H$.)
  1284	The rate table and the isolated $K_2$ values give
  1285	\[
  1286	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1287	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1288	\]
  1289	More precisely, $A/D\to Z_U$, which yields the stated limits.  The argument
  1290	uses the exact absorbing macro chain, including adverse reversals; no
  1291	branching survival probability is substituted for global fixation.
  1292	\end{proof}
  1293	
  1294	\section{The simultaneous correction and its optimization}
  1295	
  1296	Put $\eta=q/C$.  Substitution of
  1297	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB} and
  1298	Proposition~\ref{prop:gate} into the finite trace gives the key asymptotic
  1299	statement.
  1300	
  1301	\begin{proposition}[Two response functions]
  1302	\label{prop:responses}
  1303	For $C=t^4$, $q=t$, $m/q\to\lambda$, and fixed $\sigma>0$, the separated
  1304	trace satisfies
  1305	\begin{align}
  1306	 \frac{\rho^0_{\Bd,t}(r)}{\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1307	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1308	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)}
  1309	 +\frac{\lambda}{r-1}\right]+o_r(\eta),\label{eq:B}\\
  1310	 \frac{\rho^0_{\dB,t}(r)}{\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1311	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1312	 \frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}
  1313	 -\lambda\right]+o_r(\eta).\label{eq:D}
  1314	\end{align}
  1315	The remainders are uniform on every compact subinterval of $(1,\infty)$.
  1316	\end{proposition}
  1317	
  1318	\begin{proof}
  1319	The complete-graph Bd baseline differs from $p$ exponentially in $C$, while
  1320	the dB baseline differs from $p$ by $O(C^{-1})$; both errors are
  1321	$o(\eta)$.  The center terms follow from
  1322	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}.  The pair terms follow by the
  1323	exact simplifications
  1324	\begin{align}
  1325	 2\left\{\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right\}
  1320	the dB baseline differs from $p$ by $O(C^{-1})$; both errors are
  1321	$o(\eta)$.  The center terms follow from
  1322	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}.  The pair terms follow by the
  1323	exact simplifications
  1324	\begin{align}
  1325	 2\left\{\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right\}
  1326	 &=\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\\
  1327	 2\left\{\frac{[1/2]Z_D/(1+Z_D)}p-1\right\}
  1328	 &=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1329	\end{align}
  1330	One leaf replaces one core vertex and contributes
  1331	$1/p-1=1/(r-1)$ under Bd and $-1$ under dB.
  1332	All inputs are compact-uniform by Propositions~\ref{prop:weak-trace},
  1333	\ref{prop:center}, and~\ref{prop:gate}, so the combined remainders are as
  1334	stated.
  1335	\end{proof}
  1336	
  1337	\begin{lemma}[Exact tangency]
  1338	\label{lem:tangency}
  1339	At $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$, both response functions in
  1340	\eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} are strictly positive for every
  1341	$1<r<\Rhyb$ and vanish simultaneously at $r=\Rhyb$.
  1342	\end{lemma}
  1343	
  1344	\begin{proof}
  1345	Both bracketed corrections are positive precisely when
  1346	\[
  1347	 L(r,\sigma)<\lambda<U(r,\sigma),
  1348	\]
  1349	where
  1350	\[
  1351	 L=\frac{2(1-\sigma)(r-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\qquad
  1352	 U=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1353	\]
  1354	After multiplication by positive denominators, $L<U$ is equivalent to
  1355	\[
  1356	 F_r(\sigma)<0,
  1357	\]
  1358	where
  1359	\[
  1360	 F_r(\sigma)
  1361	 =(r-1)\sigma^2+(r^3-4r^2+3r+1)\sigma+r(2r-3).
  1362	\]
  1363	Its minimizing value and minimum are
  1364	\[
  1365	 \sigma(r)=\frac{-r^3+4r^2-3r-1}{2(r-1)},\qquad
  1366	 \min_\sigma F_r(\sigma)=-\frac{P(r)}{4(r-1)}.
  1367	\]
  1368	Exact Sturm counting gives no root of $P$ in $(1,3/2)$ and exactly one in
  1369	$(3/2,151/100)$.  At that root, $\sigma_*$ is the double tangency and
  1370	\[
  1371	 L(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=U(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=\lambda_*.
  1372	\]
  1373	For fixed $\sigma_*$, $L$ is increasing and $U$ decreasing on
  1374	$(1,\Rhyb)$ because
  1375	\[
  1376	 \partial_rL=
  1377	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)\{\sigma(r-1)^2-1\}}
  1378	      {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}^2}>0,\qquad
  1379	 \partial_rU=
  1380	 \frac{4r(\sigma-r)}{\{2r(r-1)+\sigma\}^2}<0.
  1381	\]
  1382	Here $0<\sigma_*<1<r$ and
  1383	$\sigma_*(r-1)^2<1$ throughout the isolating interval, so the displayed
  1384	factorizations determine the signs without numerical approximation.
  1385	Thus
  1386	\[
  1387	 L(r,\sigma_*)<\lambda_*<U(r,\sigma_*)
  1388	 \quad(1<r<\Rhyb),
  1389	\]
  1390	which proves the claim.
  1391	\end{proof}
  1392	
  1393	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
  1394	Fix $r\in(1,\Rhyb)$.  Then $r\in I_t$ for every sufficiently large $t$.
  1395	By Proposition~\ref{prop:responses} and Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}, the two
  1396	separated normalized gains, after multiplication by $n_t/q_t$, converge to
  1397	strictly positive numbers.  The defining inequality for $e_t$ changes each
  1398	scaled gain by at most $1/t$.  Thus both actual connected-graph gains are
  1399	strictly positive for all sufficiently large $t$.  The sequence, including
  1400	every weak-edge weight, was fixed before $r$ was chosen, which is exactly the
  1401	quantifier order in the definition of $\Rsim$.
  1402	\end{proof}
  1403	
  1404	\begin{proposition}[Fixed-parameter response optimality]
  1405	\label{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1406	Among fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute
  1407	pair--pendant response model \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D}, $\Rhyb$ is the exact
  1408	largest threshold of an interval beginning at one on which both corrections
  1409	can remain positive.
  1410	\end{proposition}
  1411	
  1412	\begin{proof}
  1413	For fixed positive $\sigma$, feasibility implies $F_r(\sigma)<0$, and hence
  1414	$\min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)<0$.  At $\Rhyb$ the unconstrained minimum is
  1415	zero at the admissible value $0<\sigma_*<1$.  Exact evaluation gives
  1416	\[
  1417	 P(3/2)=\frac1{64}>0,\qquad
  1418	 P(151/100)=-\frac{39866792399}{10^{12}}<0.
  1419	\]
  1420	The Sturm count in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency} shows that $\Rhyb$ is the only
  1421	root between these endpoints.  Hence $P(r)<0$ on
  1422	$(\Rhyb,151/100]$ and
  1423	$\min_sF_r(s)=-P(r)/\{4(r-1)\}>0$ there.  No positive $\sigma$---indeed no
  1424	real $\sigma$---is feasible immediately beyond $\Rhyb$.  Since the fixed
  1425	pair $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$ works throughout $(1,\Rhyb)$, no interval
  1426	beginning at one can extend farther within this response model.
  1427	\end{proof}
  1428	
  1429	\begin{corollary}[A rational-edge family]
  1430	\label{cor:rational}
  1431	Taking $\sigma=19/137$ and $\lambda=20/27$ gives, exactly at $r=3/2$,
  1432	\[
  1433	 B=\frac{232}{17361}>0,\qquad
  1434	 D=\frac{65}{12123}>0.
  1435	\]
  1436	This choice gives a family with entirely rational edge weights and algebraic
  1437	response threshold
  1438	\[
  1439	 R_{\mathbb Q}
  1440	 =\frac{5069+12\sqrt{147001}}{6439}
  1441	 =1.50176815223369\ldots>3/2.
  1442	\]
  1443	Thus a family with rational internal ratios and rational positive weak edges
  1444	already proves a strict interval beyond $3/2$.
  1445	\end{corollary}
  1446	
  1447	\begin{proof}
  1448	Substitution into \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} gives the two displayed
  1449	margins.  For these rational parameters the dB response first vanishes at
  1450	the unique root in $(1,2)$ of
  1451	\[
  1452	 6439r^2-10138r+703=0,
  1453	\]
  1454	which is $R_{\mathbb Q}$; the Bd response is still strictly positive there.
  1455	Both responses are therefore positive on $(1,R_{\mathbb Q})$ by the same
  1456	monotonicity calculation used in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}.  The weak edges
  1457	remain rational as well: repeat the least-dyadic diagonal construction with
  1458	$I_t=[1+1/t,R_{\mathbb Q}-1/t]$, $\sigma=19/137$, and
  1459	$m_t=\lfloor(20/27)t\rfloor$.  Every edge-weight ratio in this specialized
  1460	family is therefore rational.
  1461	\end{proof}
  1462	
  1463	\section{Discussion}
  1464	
  1465	The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.  One
  1466	weighted graph sequence is chosen without knowing fitness and works for each
  1467	fixed $r$ in an interval extending beyond $3/2$.  A family tuned separately
  1468	at each fitness would establish a weaker statement.  The diagonal choice of
  1469	$\eps_t$ is therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical
  1470	limit: compact convergence selects one positive cut that works
  1471	simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
  1472	
  1473	The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures.  Heavy
  1474	pairs contribute positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub
  1475	pendants contribute positively to Bd and negatively to dB.  Their densities
  1476	are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and the module
  1477	responses appear at the common scale $q_t/C_t$.  The exact Schur trace is
  1478	important here.  It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut
  1479	event and retains the possibility that a nearly successful global sweep
  1480	reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would not
  1481	prove fixation.
  1482	At the singular boundary $\sigma\downarrow0$,
  1483	$F_r(0)=r(2r-3)$ recovers the $3/2$ feasibility endpoint of this response
  1484	model; optimizing over positive $\sigma$ moves the tangency to $\Rhyb$.
  1485	
  1486	Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be
  1487	resolved: reproduction can select a parent before a replacement site, or a
  1488	vacancy can first determine which neighbors compete.  The construction
  1489	shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
  1490	occupy a special environment.  A vanishing fraction of paired niches and
  1491	hub-associated peripheral sites can bias invasion in complementary ways
  1492	while the bulk remains well mixed.  The result is qualitative rather than a
  1493	claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
  1494	structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this
  1495	beneficial-fitness regime.
  1496	
  1497	There are two sharp limitations.  Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1498	optimizes only fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed
  1499	first-order dilute response model.  It does not classify size-dependent or
  1500	singular choices $\sigma_t,\lambda_t$, different pair or pendant density
  1501	scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order boundary, nonseparated
  1502	pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions.  Conversely, the
  1503	paper supplies no finite universal upper bound on $\Rsim$.  Establishing
  1504	such a bound would require a comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on
  1505	arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a classification of this
  1506	construction.
  1507	
  1508	The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small.  Here
  1509	$q_t/C_t=t^{-3}=\Theta(n_t^{-3/4})$, so the normalized fixation gain tends to
  1510	zero.  The pair weights grow like $C_t$, while the exact diagonal may select
  1511	very small positive weak edges.  The proof gives eventual positivity for
  1512	each fixed fitness but no useful finite-size bound on $t_0(r)$.  The floor in
  1513	$m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor$ contributes $O(t^{-1})$ oscillations to the
  1514	scaled finite-$t$ responses, so positivity need not appear monotonically
  1515	before the eventual regime.  The paper makes no claim of empirical or
  1516	finite-population optimality.
  1517	
  1518	\section*{Statements and Declarations}
  1519	
  1520	\noindent\textbf{Data and code availability.}
