   277	
   278	To define the positive coupling without reference to a subsequently chosen
   279	fitness, let
   280	\[
   281	 I_t=[1+1/t,\Rhyb-1/t]
   282	\]
   283	when this interval is nonempty.  Let $\rho^0_{U,t}$ denote the finite
   284	separated trace defined in Section~\ref{sec:trace}.  Choose $e_t$ to be the
   285	least positive integer such that, for $\eps_t=2^{-e_t}$,
   286	\[
   287	 \sup_{r\in I_t}\frac{n_t}{q_t}
   288	 \left|
   289	 \frac{\rho_U(G_t(\eps_t),r)}{\rho_U(K_{n_t},r)}
   290	 -\frac{\rho^0_{U,t}(r)}{\rho_U(K_{n_t},r)}
   291	 \right|\leq\frac1t
   292	 \quad (U=\Bd,\dB).
   293	\]
   294	For the finitely many empty intervals, take $e_t=1$.
   295	
   296	\begin{lemma}[Effective dyadic diagonal]
   297	\label{lem:effective-diagonal}
   298	The integer $e_t$ exists and is computable by exact real-algebraic
   299	arithmetic.  In particular, the family $\{G_t\}$ is effectively defined and
   300	every one of its weights is fixed before fitness is quantified.
   301	\end{lemma}
   302	
   303	\begin{proof}
   304	Work over the algebraic number field $\mathbb K=\mathbb Q(\Rhyb)$.
   305	Both $\sigma_*$ and $\lambda_*$ lie in $\mathbb K$, and
   306	$m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor$ is obtained by an exact comparison of real
   307	algebraic numbers.  For fixed $t,e$ and either update rule, the transient
   308	first-step system of $G_t(2^{-e})$ has entries in $\mathbb K(r)$.  Cramer's
   309	rule therefore puts its fixation probability in $\mathbb K(r)$; the same is
   310	true of the finite separated trace and the complete-graph baseline.  For
   311	each $r\in I_t$, every transient block $Q(r)$ occurring in these absorbing
   312	systems is substochastic with spectral radius less than one.  Hence
   313	$I-Q(r)$ is a nonsingular $M$-matrix and
   314	$\det(I-Q(r))>0$; the event denominators are positive as well.
   315	
   316	After clearing those positive denominators, each of the two absolute-value
   317	conditions defining $e_t$ is a pair of polynomial inequalities over
   318	$\mathbb K$ quantified on the algebraic interval $I_t$.  Its truth is
   319	decidable by Sturm--subresultant arithmetic, equivalently by quantifier
   320	elimination in the real closure of $\mathbb K$.  Proposition
   321	\ref{prop:weak-trace} gives uniform convergence on $I_t$, so some exponent is
   322	admissible.  Testing $e=1,2,\ldots$ by the exact decision procedure therefore
   323	terminates at the least admissible $e_t$.
   324	\end{proof}
   325	
   326	\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]
   327	\label{thm:main}
   328	For every fixed $1<r<\Rhyb$, the family above satisfies, for every
   329	sufficiently large $t$,
   330	\[
   331	 \rho_{\Bd}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r),\qquad
   332	 \rho_{\dB}(G_t,r)>\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r).
   333	\]
   334	Consequently
   335	\[
   336	 \boxed{\Rsim\geq\Rhyb=1.5028569127905696\ldots>3/2.}
   337	\]
   338	\end{theorem}
   339	
   340	\section{Exact lumping and the weak-cut limiting trace}
   341	\label{sec:trace}
   342	
   343	A configuration has orbit label
   344	\[
   345	 (h,i,u,v,\ell),
   346	\]
   347	where $h$ is the hub type, $i$ the number of mutant ordinary clique
   348	vertices, $u$ the number of mixed pairs, $v$ the number of all-mutant pairs,
   349	and $\ell$ the mutant-leaf count.  The group
   350	\[
   351	S_{C_t-1}\times(S_2\wr S_{q_t})\times S_{m_t}
   352	\]
   353	is transitive on every fibre and commutes with both update kernels.  This
   354	immediately gives the following exact reduction.
   355	
   356	\begin{lemma}[Strong orbit lumping]
   357	\label{lem:lumping}
   358	For every positive value of the weak-edge weight and for both update rules,
   359	the total transition probability from a labelled configuration to a target
   360	fibre depends only on $(h,i,u,v,\ell)$.
   361	\end{lemma}
   362	
   363	\begin{proof}
   364	Every permutation in the displayed wreath-product action preserves vertex
   365	degrees, edge weights, types, and fitnesses.  It therefore commutes with each
   366	labelled parent--target event under Bd and each labelled death--parent event
   367	under dB.  Summing events into a target fibre proves strong lumpability.
   368	\end{proof}
   369	
   370	We next fix finite $C,m,q$ and send $\eps\downarrow0$.  At $\eps=0$ the
   371	closed fast classes are precisely the states in which the center module and
   372	every pair are internally homogeneous.  Write a macro state as
   373	\[
   374	 (h,k)\in\{0,1\}\times\{0,\ldots,q\},
   375	\]
   376	where $h$ is the center type and $k$ the number of mutant pairs.  Partition
   377	the finite first-step matrix into mixed-module states $F$ and all
   378	monomorphic-module states $M$, explicitly including global extinction and
   379	fixation.  Their two harmonic values remain fixed at zero and one.
   380	
   381	\begin{proposition}[Finite-state weak-cut limiting trace]
   382	\label{prop:weak-trace}
   383	Let $a_H^U,a_P^U$ be the uniformly initialized isolated fixation
   384	probabilities of the center and one pair, and let $P_U^H,P_U^P$ be fixation
   385	probabilities of the limiting macro chain from $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$.  For
   386	$U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$,
   387	\begin{equation}
   388	 \rho_U^0(C,m,q;r)
   389	 =\frac{C+m}{N}a_H^U(r)P_U^H
   390	 +\frac{2q}{N}a_P^U(r)P_U^P,
   391	 \qquad N=C+m+2q.
   392	\label{eq:finite-trace}
   393	\end{equation}
   394	Moreover, $\rho_U(G(C,m,q,\eps),r)\to\rho_U^0(C,m,q;r)$ uniformly in $r$
   395	on every compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$.
   396	\end{proposition}
   397	
   398	\begin{proof}
   399	Order the states as $M,F$.  At $\eps=0$, the first-step matrix
   400	has the block form
   401	\[
   402	 P_U(0)=
   403	 \begin{pmatrix}I&0\\ C_0&Q_0\end{pmatrix},
   404	 \qquad \rho(Q_0)<1.
   405	\]
   406	Indeed, from a mixed state each finite connected module absorbs internally
   407	almost surely, so $I-Q_0$ is invertible with nonnegative inverse and
   408	$(I-Q_0)^{-1}C_0$ is the stochastic matrix of its first monomorphic return.
   409	Expanding the positive-cut chain gives
   410	\[
   411	 P_{MM}(\eps)=I+\eps A+O(\eps^2),\qquad
   412	 P_{MF}(\eps)=\eps B+O(\eps^2),
   413	\]
   414	and $P_{FM}(\eps)=C_0+O(\eps)$,
   415	$P_{FF}(\eps)=Q_0+O(\eps)$.  Eliminating $F$ from the finite harmonic
   416	system yields, after division by the common factor $\eps$, the macro
   417	generator
   418	\begin{equation}
   419	 \mathcal L_U=A+B(I-Q_0)^{-1}C_0.
   420	\label{eq:schur-trace}
   421	\end{equation}
   422	The second term first makes one cross-module introduction and then resolves
   423	its entire zero-cut internal excursion.  Its absorption distribution is
   424	therefore exactly the affected module's local fixation probability.  On the
   425	two globally absorbing macro states we retain boundary values zero and one;
   426	on every other macro state, the leading harmonic equation is
   427	$\mathcal L_Uh=0$.  Positive introduction rates make every interior state
   428	of this finite macro ladder transient.  On a compact fitness interval its
   429	interior inverse is uniformly bounded, so the boundary-value problem has a
   430	unique solution.  This is the claimed
   431	introduction-rate-times-fixation trace, derived from the finite chain rather
   432	than from independent lineages.
   433	
   434	A uniformly chosen initial mutant first resolves inside its center or pair
   435	module with probability $1-O(\eps)$.  Conditioning on which module fixes
   436	locally gives \eqref{eq:finite-trace}.  All matrices are finite and their
   437	entries are continuous rational functions of $(\eps,r)$ with positive event
   438	denominators.  After fixing the two boundary coordinates, the scaled Schur
   439	complement converges to the invertible interior restriction of
   440	$\mathcal L_U$.  On a compact fitness interval that limiting inverse,
   441	$(I-Q_0)^{-1}$, and the nearby absorbing inverses are uniformly bounded.
   442	The block expansion and hence the convergence are uniform there.
   443	\end{proof}
   444	
   445	\section{Clique--pendant estimates at the gain scale}
   446	
   447	Let $H_{C,m}$ be a unit clique $K_C$ with $m$ unit hub pendants and put
   448	$p=1-1/r$.  Along $C=t^4$, $q=t$, and $m\asymp t$, the following estimates hold
   449	uniformly when $r$ ranges over a compact subset of $(1,\infty)$:
   450	\begin{proposition}[Center module]
   451	\label{prop:center}
   452	\begin{align}
   453	 u_{\rm core}^U(r)&=p+o(q/C),\qquad U=\Bd,\dB,\label{eq:core}\\
   454	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)&=1-o(1),\label{eq:leaf-Bd}\\
   455	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)&=O(C^{-1}).\label{eq:leaf-dB}
   456	\end{align}
   457	Here $u_{\rm core}^U$ averages the $C$ clique starting vertices.
   458	Consequently
   459	\begin{align}
   460	 a_H^{\Bd}(r)&=\frac{Cp+m}{C+m}+o(q/C),\label{eq:center-Bd}\\
   461	 a_H^{\dB}(r)&=\frac{Cp}{C+m}+o(q/C).\label{eq:center-dB}
   462	\end{align}
   463	At reciprocal fitness $1/r$,
   464	\[
   465	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),\qquad J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),
   466	\]
   467	where $J_H$ is the dB portal functional defined below.
   468	\end{proposition}
   469	
   470	We prove the proposition through five stopped-process lemmas.  This detail is
   471	needed because an $o(1)$ establishment approximation would be too weak: the
   472	final gain is only of order $q/C$.  Fix throughout a compact interval
   473	$\mathcal R=[r_-,r_+]\Subset(1,\infty)$.  Write a center state as
   474	$(h,i,\ell)$, put $c=C-1$, $d=c+m$, and suppress a common positive statewise
   475	time factor.  The six Bd changing rates are
   476	\[
   477	\begin{array}{c|c}
   478	\text{change}&\text{rate}\\ \hline
   479	i\to i+1&r(c-i)\{h/d+i/c\}\\
   480	i\to i-1&i\{(1-h)/d+(c-i)/c\}\\
   481	0\to1\text{ at }h&r(i/c+\ell)\\
   482	1\to0\text{ at }h&(c-i)/c+m-\ell\\
   483	\ell\to\ell+1&rh(m-\ell)/d\\
   484	\ell\to\ell-1&(1-h)\ell/d .
   485	\end{array}
   486	\]
   487	After multiplication by $C+m$, the six dB rates are
   488	\[
   489	\begin{array}{c|c}
   490	i\to i+1&(c-i)\dfrac{r(h+i)}{c+(r-1)(h+i)}\\[1mm]
   491	i\to i-1&i\dfrac{c-i+1-h}{c+(r-1)(i-1+h)}\\[1mm]
   492	0\to1\text{ at }h&\dfrac{r(i+\ell)}{d+(r-1)(i+\ell)}\\[1mm]
   493	1\to0\text{ at }h&\dfrac{d-i-\ell}{d+(r-1)(i+\ell)}\\[1mm]
   494	\ell\to\ell+1&h(m-\ell)\\
   495	\ell\to\ell-1&(1-h)\ell .
   496	\end{array}
   497	\]
   498	These tables follow by summing the labelled parent--target and
   499	death--parent events and are the only estimates used below.
   500	
   501	\begin{lemma}[Early ordinary-core establishment]
   502	\label{lem:early-establishment}
   503	Start from one ordinary clique mutant, with resident hub and no mutant
   504	pendants.  Let $\chi$ be the first change of the hub or pendant coordinates,
   505	let $K=\lceil A_0\log C\rceil$, and let
   506	$\tau_j=\inf\{s:i_s=j\}$ and
   507	$\tau_{0,K}=\tau_0\wedge\tau_K$.  Uniformly for
   508	$r\in\mathcal R$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$,
   509	\begin{align}
   510	 \Prob(\chi<\tau_{0,K})&=O(K/C),\label{eq:early-mark}\\
   511	 \Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)
   512	 &=1-\frac1r+O(K/C)+O(r^{-K}).\label{eq:early-est}
   513	\end{align}
   514	The constants are uniform for every fixed $A_0$.
   515	\end{lemma}
   516	
   517	\begin{proof}
   518	Until $\chi$, the exact embedded ordinary-count up/down odds are
   519	\begin{align}
   520	 R_{\Bd}(i)
   521	 &=r\,\frac{c-i}{c-i+c/(c+m)},\\
   522	 R_{\dB}(i)
   523	 &=r\,\frac{c-i}{c-i+1}
   524	 \frac{c+(r-1)(i-1)}{c+(r-1)i}.
   525	\end{align}
   526	Thus $\lambda_i^U:=(R_U(i))^{-1}=r^{-1}\{1+O(C^{-1})\}$ uniformly for
   527	$1\leq i<K$.  For large $C$, the embedded ordinary walk has conditional
   528	upward probability at least $1/2+\eta$ for some $\eta>0$ depending only on
   529	$\mathcal R$.  If $N$ is its number of changes before $\{0,K\}$, optional
   530	stopping applied to the count minus its uniform positive drift gives
   531	$\mathbb E_1N\leq K/(2\eta)=O(K)$.  At every visited state the hub-change
   532	rate is $O(i/C)$ and the ordinary-change rate is $\Theta(i)$, so their hazard
   533	ratio is $O(C^{-1})$; a union bound over
   534	the stopped changes gives \eqref{eq:early-mark}.  Pendant coordinates cannot
   535	change before the initially resident hub does.
   536	
   537	Suppressing that killing event, the product-odds formula is
   538	\[
   539	 \Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)
   540	 =\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\prod_{j=1}^k\lambda_j^U\right)^{-1}.
   541	\]
   542	For $k<K$, the product is
   543	$r^{-k}\exp\{O(k/C)\}$; summing the geometric tail and restoring the
   544	killing event changes the answer by
   545	$O(K/C)+O(r^{-K})$.  This proves \eqref{eq:early-est}.
   546	\end{proof}
   547	\begin{lemma}[Supercritical completion and core confinement]
   548	\label{lem:core-confinement}
   549	There are constants $\delta>0$, $r_0>1$, $a,b,\gamma>0$, and an integer
   550	$R_0$, depending only on $\mathcal R$, with the following properties.
   551	Uniformly in the hub and pendant coordinates, a core family starting at
   552	$i=K$ reaches $i\geq(1-\delta)c$ before extinction except with probability
   553	\[
   554	 O(r_0^{-K})+e^{-\gamma C}.
   555	\]
   556	If $R=c-i$ and $R(0)\leq\delta c$, then for every fixed $M$,
   557	\begin{equation}
   558	 \Prob\!\left(\sup_{s\leq C^M}R_s\geq2\delta c\right)
   559	 \leq C_M C^{M+1}e^{-\gamma C},
   560	\label{eq:core-confinement}
   561	\end{equation}
   562	where time is measured in the displayed continuous-time clock.  Before the
   563	exceptional exit, the bounded strip is reached with the uniform tail
   564	\begin{equation}
   565	 \Prob\bigl(\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}>a\log C+x,
   566	             \tau_{2\delta c}>\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}\bigr)
   567	 \leq a e^{-bx}.
   568	\label{eq:deficit-return}
   569	\end{equation}
   570	\end{lemma}
   571	
   572	\begin{proof}
   573	For every hub and pendant state, the ordinary-count odds remain bounded
   574	below by a constant $r_0>1$ from $K$ to a fixed positive core density.
   575	Indeed, the two tables give the uniform lower bounds
   576	\[
   577	 \frac{q^+_{\Bd}}{q^-_{\Bd}}
   578	 \geq\frac{r}{1+c/\{d(c-i)\}},\qquad
   579	 \frac{q^+_{\dB}}{q^-_{\dB}}
   580	 =r\,\frac{h+i}{i}\frac{c-i}{c-i+1-h}
   581	 \frac{c+(r-1)(i-1+h)}{c+(r-1)(i+h)}.
   582	\]
   583	For $K\to\infty$ and $i\leq(1-\delta)c$, their infimum is strictly larger
   584	than one.  This comparison remains valid although the hidden coordinates
   585	move.  At successive $i$-change epochs, condition on the complete past.  The
   586	pointwise intensity ratio implies that the conditional probability of an
   587	upward step is at least $r_0/(1+r_0)$: this follows equivalently by comparing
   588	the two compensator integrals up to the next $i$-change.  Thus
   589	$r_0^{-i_n}$ is a supermartingale for the embedded adapted process.  Stopping
   590	on $\{0,\lfloor(1-\delta)c\rfloor\}$ gives
   591	\[
   592	 \Prob_K\bigl(\tau_0<\tau_{\lfloor(1-\delta)c\rfloor}\bigr)
   593	 \leq r_0^{-K}.
   594	\]
   595	Applying the same conditional-bias comparison across any subsequent
   596	fixed-density interval gives an $e^{-\gamma C}$ failure bound.
   597	
   598	In the upper strip $1\leq R\leq2\delta c$, the same tables give, uniformly
   599	in the hub and pendant coordinates,
   600	\begin{equation}
   601	 \frac{q(R\mapsto R-1)}{q(R\mapsto R+1)}
   602	 \geq r(1-2\delta)\frac{R}{R+1}\{1-o(1)\}.
   603	\label{eq:deficit-odds}
   604	\end{equation}
   605	Writing $r_-$ for the lower end of the chosen fitness compact, choose
   606	\[
   607	 \delta<\min\left\{\frac18,\frac{r_--1}{8r_-},
   608	 \frac{r_-^2-1}{4(r_-^2+1)}\right\}.
   609	\]
   610	Then one may take $\alpha=(r_-+1)/2>1$ and, for all sufficiently large
   611	$C$, replace the right side of \eqref{eq:deficit-odds} by
   612	$\alpha R/(R+1)$.  It is larger than $1+\eta_0$ above the fixed integer
   613	$R_0=\lceil(\alpha+1)/(\alpha-1)\rceil$; no statewise drift assertion is
   614	made below $R_0$.  The hidden hub and pendant coordinates do not make the
   615	deficit marginal Markov, so we use a conditional-bias coupling rather than
   616	assuming that they do.  At successive $R$-change epochs, condition on the
   617	entire past.  Whenever $R>R_0$, the probability of an outward step divided
   618	by that of an inward step is at most $(1+\eta_0)^{-1}$.  Choose
   619	$0<\theta<\log(1+\eta_0)$.  Then
   620	\[
   621	 \exp\{\theta R_{n\wedge\tau}\},\qquad
   622	 \tau=\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}},
   623	\]
   624	is a supermartingale for the embedded adapted process.  Optional stopping
   625	therefore gives, uniformly in all hidden-coordinate histories,
   626	\begin{equation}
   627	 \sup_{k\leq\delta c}
   628	 \Prob_k(\tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}<\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}})
   629	 \leq e^{-\theta\delta c}=e^{-\gamma C}.
   630	\label{eq:deficit-initial-excursion}
   631	\end{equation}
   632	The same estimate, started at $R_0+1$, controls every later excursion.
   633	The total $R$-change intensity in the displayed clock is at most $\Lambda C$.
   634	Its compensator through time $C^M$ is therefore at most
   635	$\Lambda C^{M+1}$.  Applying the strong Markov property at successive
   636	entries into $R_0+1$ and multiplying the last excursion bound by the expected
   637	number of entries proves \eqref{eq:core-confinement}; Markov's inequality for
   638	the compensator already suffices.
   639	
   640	For completeness, the stopped generator calculation behind the return-time
   641	claim is also explicit.  Substituting $i=c-R$ in the two tables gives
   642	\begin{align*}
   643	 \mathcal G_{\Bd}R
   644	 &=(c-R)\left\{\frac{1-h}{d}+\frac Rc\right\}
   645	   -rR\left\{\frac hd+\frac{c-R}{c}\right\},\\
   646	 \mathcal G_{\dB}R
   647	 &=(c-R)\frac{R+1-h}{c+(r-1)(c-R-1+h)}
   648	   -R\frac{r(c-R+h)}{c+(r-1)(c-R+h)}.
   649	\end{align*}
   650	After increasing $R_0$ and decreasing $\delta$ if necessary, both are at
   651	most $-\kappa R$ for $R_0<R<2\delta c$, uniformly in $h,\ell$, and
   652	$r\in\mathcal R$.  Thus
   653	$e^{\kappa(s\wedge\tau)}R_{s\wedge\tau}$ is a supermartingale in the
   654	displayed clock.  On the event in \eqref{eq:deficit-return} it has not yet
   655	hit either boundary at time $a\log C+x$.  Hence
   656	\[
   657	 \Prob(\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}}>a\log C+x,
   658	       \tau_{\{R\geq2\delta c\}}>\tau_{\{R\leq R_0\}})
   659	 \leq \frac{\delta C}{R_0}e^{-\kappa(a\log C+x)}.
   660	\]
   661	Taking $a>2/\kappa$ proves \eqref{eq:deficit-return}.  The states below
   662	$R_0$ form the announced boundary layer and require no statewise drift claim.
   663	\end{proof}
   664	
   665	\begin{lemma}[Pendant synchronization and pendant initialization]
   666	\label{lem:pendant-cleanup}
   667	For every fixed $B_0>0$ there is a fixed $D_{\rm cl}=D_{\rm cl}(B_0)$ such
   668	that, under either update
   669	rule and uniformly in $r\in\mathcal R$, a state with
   670	$i\geq(1-\delta)c$ reaches fixation within time $C^{D_{\rm cl}}$, except with
   671	probability
   672	\[
   673	 O(C^{-B_0})+O(C^{D_{\rm cl}+1}e^{-\gamma C}).
   674	\]
   675	Moreover, fixation from one mutant pendant satisfies
   676	\[
   677	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1),\qquad
   678	 u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1}).
   679	\]
   680	\end{lemma}
   681	
   682	\begin{proof}
   683	We first synchronize the hub and its pendants while the core is confined.  Put
   684	$\tau_\uparrow=\inf\{s\geq0:R_s\geq2\delta c\}$.  Under Bd, freeze
   685	$(i,R,\ell)$ between hub and pendant events and put
   686	\[
   687	 H_+=r(i/c+\ell),\quad H_-=R/c+m-\ell,
   688	 \quad U_\ell=\frac{r(m-\ell)}{c+m},\quad
   689	 V_\ell=\frac{\ell}{c+m}.
   690	\]
   691	If $p_h$ is the probability that the next leaf event is upward, conditional
   692	on the current hub type $h$, renewal at the next hub flip gives
   693	\[
   694	 p_0=\frac{H_+}{H_++V_\ell}p_1,\qquad
   695	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell}{H_-+U_\ell}
   696	     +\frac{H_-}{H_-+U_\ell}p_0.
   697	\]
   698	To make the interruption comparison formal, replace these rates by
   699	\[
   700	 \underline H_+=r_-(1-2\delta+\ell),\qquad
   701	 \overline H_-=2\delta+m-\ell,
   702	 \qquad \underline U_\ell=\frac{r_-(m-\ell)}{c+m},
   703	\]
   704	and let $(\underline p_0,\underline p_1)$ solve the same two equations with
   705	these worst rates and $V_\ell$.  Extend this two-state hitting function by
   706	giving an upper-strip exit the boundary value one.  The resulting function is
   707	subharmonic for the full $(h,i,\ell)$ generator until the next pendant-count
   708	change or $\tau_\uparrow$: core jumps inside the strip leave it unchanged, a
   709	jump through the upper boundary can only increase it, and
   710	$H_+\geq\underline H_+$, $H_-\leq\overline H_-$, and
   711	$U_\ell\geq\underline U_\ell$ move its generator in the favorable direction.
   712	Indeed, the two renewal equations give
   713	\[
   714	 p_0=\frac{H_+U_\ell}
   715	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)},\qquad
   716	 p_1=\frac{U_\ell(H_++V_\ell)}
   717	 {H_+U_\ell+V_\ell(H_-+U_\ell)};
   718	\]
   719	direct differentiation shows that both are increasing in $H_+$ and
   720	$U_\ell$ and decreasing in $H_-$.  This proves the asserted favorable
   721	monotonicity rather than leaving it implicit.
   722	Optional stopping therefore makes it a lower bound for the probability that
   723	the next stopped outcome is an upward pendant change or upper-strip exit, even
   724	with an adapted sequence of core interruptions.  For $1\leq\ell<m$, its
   725	up-or-exit/down odds, from either hub phase, are bounded below by
   726	\begin{equation}
   727	 \frac{\underline H_+\underline U_\ell}
   728	      {\overline H_-V_\ell}
   729	 \frac1{1+\underline U_\ell/\overline H_-}
   730	 \geq\frac{r_-^2}{1+2\delta}\{1-o(1)\}>1.
   731	\label{eq:leaf-trace-odds}
   732	\end{equation}
   733	
   734	We now keep the same stopping boundary in the calendar-time estimate.  For an
   735	initial state with $\ell<m$, let
   736	$\xi=\tau_{\{\ell=m\}}\wedge\tau_\uparrow$, put $\sigma_0=0$, and, while
   737	$\sigma_j<\xi$, set
   738	\[
   739	 \sigma_{j+1}
   740	 =\inf\{s>\sigma_j:\ell_s\ne\ell_{\sigma_j}\}\wedge\tau_\uparrow.
   741	\]
   742	Assign the stopped trace the level
   743	$\widehat\ell_j=\ell_{\sigma_j}$ when $\sigma_j<\tau_\uparrow$ and the level
   744	$\widehat\ell_j=m$ when $\sigma_j=\tau_\uparrow$, and keep it at $m$
   745	thereafter.  Put $N=\inf\{j:\widehat\ell_j=m\}$.  The stopped committor
   746	comparison above gives a fixed $\beta>1$ such that, on $\{j<N\}$, the
   747	conditional up-or-exit/down odds of $\widehat\ell$ are at least $\beta$; at
   748	$\ell=0$ a downward move is impossible.  Thus, with
   749	$\varepsilon=(\beta-1)/(\beta+1)>0$,
   750	$\widehat\ell_{j\wedge N}-\varepsilon(j\wedge N)$ is a submartingale.
   751	Optional stopping at $N\wedge k$ gives
   752	\[
   753	 \varepsilon\,\mathbb E(N\wedge k)
   754	 \leq\mathbb E\widehat\ell_{N\wedge k}-\ell_0\leq m,
   755	\]
   756	and monotone convergence gives $\mathbb EN\leq m/\varepsilon=O(m)$.
   757	
   758	It remains to convert stopped trace changes into calendar time.  Before
   759	$\tau_\uparrow$, during an $h=0$ phase the activation-or-loss intensity is
   760	bounded below by a positive constant, and during an $h=1$ phase with
   761	$\ell<m$ the deactivation-or-gain intensity is at least
   762	$m-\ell\geq1$.  Moreover,
   763	\begin{align*}
   764	 \Prob(\text{loss or upper exit before activation}\mid h=0)
   765	 &\geq \frac{V_\ell}{r_+(1+\ell)+V_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},
   766	 &&1\leq\ell<m,\\
   767	 \Prob(\text{gain or upper exit before deactivation}\mid h=1)
   768	 &\geq\frac{\underline U_\ell}
   769	 {2\delta+m-\ell+\underline U_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},
   770	 &&0\leq\ell<m.
   771	\end{align*}
   772	At $\ell=0$ a resident-hub phase has no loss, but it ends by activation (or
   773	upper exit), after which the mutant-hub bound applies.  Core jumps contribute
   774	zero to functions of $(h,\ell)$ and do not pause these competing clocks.
   775	Consequently, grouping at most two consecutive hub phases, the number of
   776	phases before the next pendant change or upper-strip exit has conditional mean
   777	$O(C)$, and every phase has uniformly bounded conditional mean duration.
   778	Thus, on $\{j<N\}$,
   779	\begin{equation}
   780	 \mathbb E(\sigma_{j+1}-\sigma_j\mid\mathcal F_{\sigma_j})
   781	 \leq K_0C,
   782	\label{eq:leaf-wait}
   783	\end{equation}
   784	and summing over $j<N$ yields
   785	$\mathbb E\xi\leq K_0C\,\mathbb EN=O(Cm)$.  If initially
   786	$(h,\ell)=(0,m)$, activation, pendant loss, or upper-strip exit occurs in
   787	bounded mean time.  The probability that loss occurs first is $O(C^{-1})$;
   788	activation or exit stops the synchronization, while after a loss the preceding
   789	trace starts from $m-1$.  Therefore, for
   790	\[
   791	 \Sigma=\inf\{s\geq0:(h_s,\ell_s)=(1,m)
   792	                         \text{ or }R_s\geq2\delta c\},
   793	\]
   794	\begin{equation}
   795	 \sup_{\substack{0\leq R<2\delta c,\ h\in\{0,1\}\\0\leq\ell\leq m}}
   796	 \mathbb E_{h,R,\ell}\Sigma=O(Cm).
   797	\label{eq:leaf-hitting-time}
   798	\end{equation}
   799	
   800	On the nonexceptional event $\{\Sigma<\tau_\uparrow\}$ the process is at
   801	$(h,\ell)=(1,m)$.  First suppress hub loss and keep $\ell=m$.  The Bd deficit
   802	generator is then exactly
   803	\[
   804	 \mathcal G R
   805	 =-R\left\{\frac r{c+m}+(r-1)\left(1-\frac Rc\right)\right\}.
   806	\]
   807	After shrinking $\delta$ once, the bracket is at least a compact-uniform
   808	$\kappa>0$ on $R\leq3\delta c$.  Starting from $R\leq2\delta c$ and stopping
   809	at $\tau=\tau_0\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}$, Dynkin's formula gives
   810	\[
   811	 \mathbb E\int_0^\tau R_s\,ds\leq\frac{R(0)}\kappa
   812	 \leq\frac{2\delta c}\kappa.
   813	\]
   814	The same adapted step calculation, now with $3\delta$ in place of
   815	$2\delta$, also gives
   816	$\Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}<\tau_0)\leq e^{-\gamma C}$.  Conditional
   817	on the suppressed path, the probability of no hub loss is
   818	$\exp\{-\int_0^\tau R_s/c\,ds\}$.  Jensen's inequality and subtraction of
   819	the upper-exit event therefore yield
   820	\begin{equation}
   821	 \Prob(\hbox{no hub loss and }\tau_0<\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}})
   822	 \geq e^{-2\delta/\kappa}-e^{-\gamma C}=:s_1+o(1)>0.
   823	\label{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}
   824	\end{equation}
   825	The same exponential Lyapunov estimate makes this final cleanup occur within
   826	$C^2$ with probability $1-o(1)$.  Together with
   827	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}, $m=o(C)$, Markov's inequality, and
   828	\eqref{eq:core-confinement}, this gives a fixed $s_0>0$ chance of full cleanup
   829	in a block of $2C^2$ time.  If hub loss ends
   830	an unsuccessful final attempt, the same adapted deficit comparison, now
   831	using the nested strips $2\delta c<3\delta c<4\delta c$ after shrinking
   832	$\delta$, returns the process to $R\leq\delta c$ in polynomial time before
   833	the next block; escape to $4\delta c$ before that return costs
   834	$e^{-\gamma C}$.  Starting the next block from $R\leq\delta c$ puts it back
   835	under the $2\delta c$ confinement estimate.  The earlier
   836	hit of $3\delta c$ in \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup} is charged to this same
   837	exceptional event rather than recycled as a new block.  We now formalize the
   838	repetition.
   839	
   840	Here is the stopping-time regeneration behind that last sentence.  Put
   841	$\mathcal E_C=\{R\leq\delta c\}$, write $\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}$ for the
   842	fixation time, and let $S_1$ be the initial time (or the first entry into
   843	$\mathcal E_C$).  Given $S_j$, run the following block:
   844	put
   845	\[
   846	 \Sigma_j=\inf\{s\geq S_j:(h_s,\ell_s)=(1,m)
   847	                         \text{ or }R_s\geq2\delta c\}.
   848	\]
   849	The stopped synchronization estimate \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time} gives
   850	$\mathbb E(\Sigma_j-S_j\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})\leq K C m$, with a hit of
   851	$2\delta c$ charged as exceptional.  From a nonexceptional $\Sigma_j$, run
   852	the final cleanup until fixation, hub loss, or a hit of $3\delta c$;
   853	and, after an ordinary hub-loss failure below $3\delta c$, wait for the
   854	first return to $\mathcal E_C$, charging a hit of $4\delta c$ as exceptional.
   855	Let $T_j$ be the time of fixation, exceptional escape, or this return.  On
   856	an ordinary return set $S_{j+1}=T_j$; a hit of $3\delta c$ is charged rather
   857	than recycled.  For that return, shrink $\delta$ so that
   858	$\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ on $\delta c<R<4\delta c$, and put
   859	$\rho=\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}}\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}$.
   860	The same adapted-step and generator arguments give, from $R<3\delta c$,
   861	\[
   862	 \Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq4\delta c\}}<\tau_{\{R\leq\delta c\}})
   863	 \leq e^{-\gamma C},\qquad
   864	 \Prob(\rho>t)\leq3e^{-\kappa t}.
   865	\]
   866	Equations
   867	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}, \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}, and
   868	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}, together with this nested-strip estimate, give
   869	constants $s_*>0$, $D_*=D_*(B_0)$, and $\gamma_*>0$ such that, uniformly on
   870	$\mathcal F_{S_j}$,
   871	\begin{align}
   872	 \Prob(\hbox{fixation during block }j,\ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}
   873	       \mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   874	 &\geq s_*,\label{eq:Bd-block-success}\\
   875	 \Prob(\hbox{upper-strip escape during block }j,
   876	       \ T_j-S_j\leq C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   877	 &\leq K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C},\label{eq:Bd-block-escape}\\
   878	 \Prob(T_j-S_j>C^{D_*}\mid\mathcal F_{S_j})
   879	 &\leq C^{-B_0-2}.
   880	 \label{eq:Bd-block-duration}
   881	\end{align}
   882	For the last bound, the synchronization part has conditional mean $O(Cm)$
   883	by \eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}; the final attempt and return part has
   884	conditional mean $O(C^2)$ by the stopped Lyapunov estimates.  Markov's
   885	inequality gives \eqref{eq:Bd-block-duration} after taking, for example,
   886	$D_*=B_0+4$.  With
   887	\[
   888	 N_C=\left\lceil
   889	 \frac{(B_0+2)\log C}{-\log(1-s_*)}\right\rceil,
   890	\]
   891	the strong Markov property at the $S_j$ gives
   892	\[
   893	 \Prob(\tau_{\mathrm{fix}}>N_CC^{D_*})
   894	 \leq(1-s_*)^{N_C}
   895	   +N_C\{C^{-B_0-2}+K C^{D_*+1}e^{-\gamma_*C}\}
   896	 =O(C^{-B_0}).
   897	\]
   898	Moreover $N_CC^{D_*}\leq C^{D_{\rm cl}}$ for a fixed
   899	$D_{\rm cl}=D_{\rm cl}(B_0)$.  This proves the
   900	claimed Bd time and failure bounds without an implicit independence
   901	assumption between blocks.
   902	
   903	Under dB, every pendant copies the hub at its next death.  In the bounded
   904	$R$ strip, first wait for $R\leq R_0$ using
   905	\eqref{eq:deficit-return}.  The hub activation rate is then bounded below.
   906	During a mutant-hub interval, its deactivation intensity is at most
   907	$K(R+m-\ell)/C$.  Suppressing that deactivation, direct substitution in the
   908	dB table gives $\mathcal G R\leq-\kappa R$ while $R\leq2\delta c$, and the
   909	number $m-\ell$ of resident pendants is a pure-death chain with drift
   910	$-(m-\ell)$.  Choose a fixed $\beta_0=\beta_0(B_0)>0$ such that
   911	\[
   912	 \beta_0-\frac14\geq B_0+2,
   913	 \qquad
   914	 \kappa\beta_0\geq B_0+2,
   915	\]
   916	and put $T=\beta_0\log C$.  Then
   917	\begin{align*}
   918	 \Prob(\ell_T<m)&\leq me^{-T},\\
   919	 \Prob(R_T>0,\ \tau_\uparrow>T)
   920	 &\leq R_0e^{-\kappa T},\\
   921	 \Prob(\text{hub deactivates before }T\wedge\tau_\uparrow)
   922	 &\leq \mathbb E\int_0^{T\wedge\tau_\uparrow}
   923	 K\frac{R_s+m-\ell_s}{C}\,ds
   924	   \leq\frac K C\left(\frac{R_0}{\kappa}+m\right)=o(1).
   925	\end{align*}
   926	The upper-strip exit at the already defined $\tau_\uparrow$ is omitted from
   927	these displays and has probability $e^{-\gamma C}$.  Since $m=O(C^{1/4})$,
   928	the choice of $\beta_0$ makes the first two displayed probabilities
   929	$O(C^{-B_0-2})$,
   930	whereas hub survival has probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus an activated attempt
   931	started in $R\leq R_0$ succeeds with
   932	probability at least $1/2$ for large $C$.  After a failed attempt, confinement
   933	and \eqref{eq:deficit-return} return the process to $R\leq R_0$ in
   934	polynomial time; unless fixation has already occurred, the hub is activated
   935	again at a rate bounded below.  The strong Markov property over
   936	$O_{B_0}(\log C)$ such attempts gives failure $O(C^{-B_0})$, while their
   937	total duration is polynomial.  Taking $D_{\rm cl}$ larger than the two block
   938	exponents and using \eqref{eq:core-confinement} proves the first assertion.
   939	
   940	For a Bd mutant leaf, use the standard weighted graphical construction: each
   941	vertex $u$ has a base reproduction clock of rate one and, when it rings,
   942	targets $v$ with probability $w_{uv}/d_u$; a mutant $u$ has an independent
   943	extra clock of rate $r-1$ with the same target law.  Deleting mutants can
   944	only decrease the mutant set.  Fix the original mutant pendant.  At every
   945	regeneration with resident hub, no ordinary mutants, and that pendant still
   946	alive, delete all other mutants and suppress births to the other pendants.
   947	This produces the adverse lower state $(h,i,\ell)=(0,0,1)$.
   948	In its resident-hub phase, activation has rate $r$ and loss of the
   949	distinguished pendant has rate $1/d$, so loss before activation is
   950	$O(C^{-1})$.  In the ensuing mutant-hub phase, an ordinary seed has rate at
   951	least $rc/d\geq a_0$, while hub loss has rate $m$; hence a seed precedes hub
   952	loss with probability at least $a_1/m$.
   953	
   954	After a seed, stop the ordinary count on $\{0,K\}$.  For every hidden hub
   955	and pendant state, the Bd up/down odds on $1\leq i<K$ are at least a fixed
   956	$r_1>1$.  The conditional-bias argument therefore gives
   957	$\Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)\geq p_0>0$.  The embedded walk makes $O(K)$ expected
   958	changes, and its changing intensity is bounded below, so
   959	$\mathbb E\tau_{0,K}=O(K)$.  The distinguished-pendant loss intensity is at
   960	most $1/d$, giving $O(K/C)$ loss during this stopped trial.  If the count
   961	hits zero while the pendant survives, wait for resident-hub regeneration,
   962	suppressing or deleting every ordinary reseed and every additional mutant
   963	pendant until hub loss.  This is again an adverse graphical coupling: the
   964	distinguished pendant cannot be lost while its sole neighbor is mutant, and
   965	any subsequent resident-phase loss is charged as trial failure.  Delete the
   966	extra mutants again at regeneration.  If it hits $K$, the preceding completion
   967	argument fixes with probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus a trial succeeds with
   968	probability at least $a_2/m$, whereas it loses the distinguished pendant
   969	with probability $O((1+K)/C)$.  The elementary renewal inequality for these
   970	competing outcomes gives loss before success
   971	$O(m(1+K)/C)=o(1)$.  Hence $u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1)$.
   972	Under dB, the leaf dies at rate one after the common death-clock time change,
   973	whereas hub activation is at most $r_+/C$.  Fixation requires that activation,
   974	so $u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1})$.
   975	\end{proof}
   976	
   977	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal killed-Green bounds]
   978	\label{lem:killed-green}
   979	Put $s=1/r$ and suppress hub changes while $h=0,\ell=0$.  Stop the resulting
   980	ordinary-count chain $I$ on hitting $0$ or $\lceil\delta c\rceil$, and define
   981	\[
   982	 G_C^U(i,j)=\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
   983	             \mathbf1_{\{I_z=j\}}\,dz.
   984	\]
   985	For every fixed $L$ there are $a_L,\kappa,\theta>0$, uniform for
   986	$s\in[1/r_+,1/r_-]$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$, such that
   987	\begin{align}
   988	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i(\tau_{\lceil\delta c\rceil}<\tau_0)
   989	 &\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C},\label{eq:green-macro}\\
   990	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_jjG_C^U(i,j)&\leq a_L,
   991	 \label{eq:green-mass}\\
   992	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_{j>L'}jG_C^U(i,j)
   993	 &\leq a_Le^{-\theta L'}.
   994	 \label{eq:green-tail}
   995	\end{align}
   996	Consequently the probability of a hub change before ordinary extinction is
   997	$O_L(C^{-1})$, and the ordinary count at that change has an exponentially
   998	decreasing tail after multiplication by $C$.
   999	\end{lemma}
  1000	
  1001	\begin{proof}
  1002	For $i\leq\delta c$, the reciprocal-fitness versions of the two rate tables
  1003	give
  1004	\[
  1005	 \frac{q_i^+}{q_i^-}\leq s_+\{1+O(\delta)+O(C^{-1})\}\leq\vartheta<1
  1006	\]
  1007	after decreasing $\delta$, where $s_+=1/r_-$.  They also give
  1008	$q_i^--q_i^+\geq\kappa_0i$ in the displayed clock.  The product-odds formula
  1009	for the dominated nearest-neighbor walk proves \eqref{eq:green-macro}.
  1010	Dynkin's formula for $I_{z\wedge\tau}$ gives
  1011	\[
  1012	 \kappa_0\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_z\,dz\leq i,
  1013	\]
  1014	which is \eqref{eq:green-mass}.  To obtain \eqref{eq:green-tail}, let $\nu$
  1015	be the first hit of $L'+1$.  Conditional bias gives
  1016	$\Prob_i(\nu<\tau_0)\leq K_L\vartheta^{L'-L}$.  After $\nu$, the same Dynkin
  1017	estimate bounds the expected remaining particle-time by
  1018	$(L'+1)/\kappa_0$.  Therefore
  1019	\[
  1020	 \mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
  1021	 \leq \frac{K_L(L'+1)}{\kappa_0}\vartheta^{L'-L}
  1022	 \leq a_Le^{-\theta L'},
  1023	\]
  1024	after decreasing $\theta$.
  1025	
  1026	With resident hub and no mutant pendants, the hub-change intensity in either
  1027	rule is at most $K_1I_z/C$.  If $\chi$ is that first change, the two precise
  1028	consequences are
  1029	\begin{align}
  1030	 \Prob_i(\chi<\tau)&\leq \frac{K_1a_L}{C},\label{eq:green-hub-change}\\
  1031	 \Prob_i(\chi<\tau,\ I_\chi>L')
  1032	 &\leq\frac{K_1}{C}\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
  1033	 I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
  1034	 \leq\frac{K_1a_L}{C}e^{-\theta L'}.
  1035	\label{eq:green-hub-tail}
  1036	\end{align}
  1037	This proves the asserted change-event bounds.
  1038	\end{proof}
  1039	
  1040	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal hub-excursion renewal]
  1041	\label{lem:reciprocal-renewal}
  1042	Uniformly for $r\in\mathcal R$,
  1043	\[
  1044	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),\qquad
  1045	 J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1}).
  1046	\]
  1047	\end{lemma}
  1048	
  1049	\begin{proof}
  1050	For fixed $L$, let $X_L$ be the states with resident hub, no mutant pendants,
  1051	and at most $L$ mutant ordinary vertices; let $Y_L$ be the analogous states
  1052	with mutant hub.  Write $x^U_{C,L}$ and $y^U_{C,L}$ for the largest fixation
  1053	probabilities on these two sets.  Lemma~\ref{lem:killed-green} first gives
  1054	the coarse bound
  1055	\begin{equation}
  1056	 x^U_{C,L}\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C}+a_L/C=O_L(C^{-1}).
  1057	\label{eq:renewal-coarse}
  1058	\end{equation}
  1059	
  1060	We now make the hub-excursion stopping decomposition explicit.  For Bd,
  1061	start from $Y_L$.  The hub-loss rate is at least $m$, while the total
  1062	ordinary-changing rate is $O_L(1)$ and the pendant-seeding rate is $O(m/C)$.
  1063	If hub loss is the first such event, the return state lies in $X_L$ exactly;
  1064	charging every other first event as successful fixation gives
  1065	\begin{equation}
  1066	 y^{\Bd}_{C,L}\leq x^{\Bd}_{C,L}+K_Lm^{-1}+K C^{-1}=o(1).
  1067	\label{eq:renewal-y-Bd}
  1068	\end{equation}
  1069	
  1070	For dB, let $\zeta$ be the first hub loss.  Until the ordinary count reaches
  1071	$\delta c$, the hub-loss intensity is bounded below explicitly:
  1072	\[
  1073	 q_{h:1\mapsto0}
  1074	 =\frac{d-i-\ell}{d+(s-1)(i+\ell)}
  1075	 \geq\frac{(1-\delta)c}{d}=:k_h>0.
  1076	\]
  1077	While the hub is mutant, the ordinary count is dominated by a
  1078	subcritical birth--death process with immigration:
  1079	\[
  1080	 q_i^+\leq b(i+1),\qquad q_i^-\geq d_0i,\qquad b<d_0,
  1081	\]
  1082	after decreasing $\delta$.  A common-clock monotone coupling gives
  1083	$I_s\leq\bar I_s$ for $s\leq\zeta$ until the macro boundary, where $\bar I$ has
  1084	birth rate $b(i+1)$ and death rate $d_0i$.  To make its maximum bound
  1085	explicit, choose $1<z<d_0/b$ and put $V(i)=z^i$.  Its generator satisfies
  1086	\[
  1087	 \overline{\mathcal G}V(i)
  1088	 =(z-1)z^i\left[b-\left(\frac{d_0}{z}-b\right)i\right]
  1089	 \leq K_z-\alpha_zV(i)
  1090	\]
  1091	for suitable compact-uniform $K_z,\alpha_z>0$.  If
  1092	$\bar\tau_M=\inf\{s:\bar I_s\geq M\}$, Dynkin's formula stopped at
  1093	$T\wedge\bar\tau_M$ gives
  1094	\begin{equation}
  1095	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i\!\left(\sup_{s\leq T}\bar I_s\geq M\right)
  1096	 \leq (z^L+K_zT)z^{-M}.
  1097	\label{eq:immigration-maximum}
  1098	\end{equation}
  1099	Consequently the ordinary cloud before $\zeta$ is uniformly tight.
  1100	Indeed, for fixed $T$ and $L\leq L_1<\delta c$,
  1101	\[
  1102	 \Prob\!\left(\sup_{s<\zeta}I_s>L_1\right)
  1103	 \leq e^{-k_hT}+(z^L+K_zT)z^{-L_1}
  1104	 =:\eta_L(L_1,T),
  1105	\]
  1106	because on the event that the cloud stays below $L_1$ through time $T$, the
  1107	hub-loss intensity remains at least $k_h$ throughout.  Thus the first term
  1108	is precisely the surviving-hub part of the finite-$T$ split; no
  1109	infinite-horizon maximum estimate is being used.
  1110	For fixed $T$, let $L_1\to\infty$ and then let $T\to\infty$; the bound
  1111	vanishes in that order,
  1112	uniformly in $C$ and reciprocal fitness.
  1113	
  1114	On the complementary event, suppress hub reactivation after $\zeta$.
  1115	The mutant pendants are then pure deaths, so
  1116	$\mathbb E\int_0^\infty\ell_s\,ds\leq m$; the killed-Green bound gives
  1117	$\mathbb E\int_0^\tau I_s\,ds\leq a_{L_1}$.  Since the hub-reactivation
  1118	intensity is at most $K(I_s+\ell_s)/C$, its probability before pendant
  1119	clearance and ordinary extinction is at most
  1120	$K(m+a_{L_1})/C$.  Conditional bias also gives
  1121	\[
  1122	 \Prob_{\{I\leq L_1\}}(\tau_{L_2}<\tau_0)
  1123	 \leq K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}.
  1124	\]
  1125	If neither event occurs, the process enters $X_{L_2}$.  Restoring the
  1126	suppressed reactivation only adds its displayed probability, so the strong
  1127	Markov property yields
  1128	\begin{equation}
  1129	 y^{\dB}_{C,L}
  1130	 \leq \eta_L(L_1,T)+\frac{K(m+a_{L_1})}{C}
  1131	      +K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}+x^{\dB}_{C,L_2}.
  1132	\label{eq:renewal-y-dB}
  1133	\end{equation}
  1134	For fixed $L_1,L_2,T$, let $C\to\infty$; then let $L_2\to\infty$,
  1135	$L_1\to\infty$, and $T\to\infty$ in that order.  Equations
  1136	\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} prove
  1137	$y^U_{C,L}=o(1)$ for each fixed $L$.
  1138	
  1139	Return now to one ordinary mutant.  At its first hub change, the explicit
  1140	hazard-weighted estimates \eqref{eq:green-macro} and
  1141	\eqref{eq:green-hub-tail} give a constant $a$ such that
  1142	\begin{equation}
  1143	 x^U_{C,1}\leq a e^{-\kappa C}
  1144	 +\frac aC\bigl(y^U_{C,L}+e^{-\theta L}\bigr).
  1145	\label{eq:renewal-refined}
  1146	\end{equation}
  1147	Therefore
  1148	$\limsup_{C\to\infty}Cx^U_{C,1}\leq ae^{-\theta L}$; letting $L\to\infty$
  1149	proves $x^U_{C,1}=o(C^{-1})$, while
  1150	\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} give
  1151	$y^U_{C,0}=o(1)$.  Hence
  1152	\begin{align*}
  1153	 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)
  1154	 &\leq\frac{C-1}{C}x^{\Bd}_{C,1}+\frac1Cy^{\Bd}_{C,0}
  1155	 =o(C^{-1}),\\
  1156	 J_H(1/r)
  1157	 &\leq x^{\dB}_{C,1}+\frac{y^{\dB}_{C,0}}{c+m}
  1158	 =o(C^{-1}),
  1159	\end{align*}
  1160	because every ordinary clique vertex has degree $c$ and the hub has degree
  1161	$c+m$.
  1162	\end{proof}
  1163	
  1164	\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:center}]
  1165	Choose $A_0$ so that both $r^{-K}$ and $r_0^{-K}$ in
  1166	Lemmas~\ref{lem:early-establishment} and \ref{lem:core-confinement} are
  1167	$o(q/C)=o(C^{-3/4})$, and then choose $B_0>3/4$ in
  1168	Lemma~\ref{lem:pendant-cleanup}.  Since
  1169	\[
  1170	 K/C=o(C^{-3/4}),\qquad C^{-B_0}=o(C^{-3/4}),
  1171	\]
  1172	the early-establishment probability is $p+o(q/C)$ and every established
  1173	family completes fixation with failure $o(q/C)$.  The hub starting vertex
  1174	has normalized mass $C^{-1}=o(q/C)$.  This proves \eqref{eq:core};
  1175	Lemma~\ref{lem:pendant-cleanup} proves \eqref{eq:leaf-Bd} and
  1176	\eqref{eq:leaf-dB}; and Lemma~\ref{lem:reciprocal-renewal} proves the two
  1177	reciprocal estimates.
  1178	
  1179	Finally, average the $C-1$ ordinary starts, the hub start, and the $m$
  1180	pendant starts.  Their contributions give
  1181	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}; the exceptional hub mass is
  1182	$O(C^{-1})=o(q/C)$.
  1183	\end{proof}
  1184	
  1185	\section{Pair gates and the complete post-establishment path}
  1186	
  1187	For the center, let
  1188	\[
  1189	 I_H=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}\frac1{d_x}
  1190	 =1+\frac1{C-1+m},
  1191	\qquad
  1192	 J_H(r)=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}
  1193	 \frac{h_H^{\dB}(\{x\};r)}{d_x},
  1194	\]
  1195	where $h_H^{\dB}$ is the isolated-center dB fixation committor.
  1196	For a pair with internal weight $W=C/\sigma$, put
  1197	\[
  1198	 I_P=\frac2W=\frac{2\sigma}{C},\qquad
  1199	 J_P(r)=\frac1W=\frac{\sigma}{C}.
  1200	\]
  1201	The second identity uses the exact dB singleton value $1/2$ on $K_2$.
  1202	The center estimates imply
  1203	\[
  1204	 I_H=1+o(1),\qquad J_H(r)=p+o(1),
  1205	\]
  1206	while $J_H(1/r)$ and the reciprocal core fixation probability are
  1207	$o(C^{-1})$.
  1208	
  1209	\begin{proposition}[Gate odds and global sweep]
  1210	\label{prop:gate}
  1211	For every fixed $r>1$, the separated macro-fixation probabilities satisfy
  1212	\[
  1213	 P_U^H=1-o(q/C),
  1214	 \qquad
  1215	 P_{\Bd}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_B}{1+Z_B},
  1216	 \qquad
  1217	 P_{\dB}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_D}{1+Z_D},
  1218	\]
  1219	where
  1220	\[
  1221	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1222	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1223	\]
  1224	The convergence is uniform when $r$ ranges over a compact subinterval of
  1225	$(1,\infty)$.
  1226	\end{proposition}
  1227	
  1228	\begin{proof}
  1229	For one specified pair, let $A,D,B,C'$ denote, respectively, mutant-pair
  1230	invasion of a resident center, resident-center recovery of that pair,
  1231	mutant-center invasion of a resident pair, and resident-pair recovery of
  1232	the center.  In each row below $a_P$ means the isolated pair fixation
  1233	$a_P^U$ for that update rule.  Summing the parent--target or death--parent events gives,
  1234	after a common time change,
  1235	\[
  1236	\begin{array}{c|cccc}
  1237	 &A&D&B&C'\\ \hline
  1238	 \Bd&
  1239	 CrI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(r)&
  1240	 2I_Ha_P(1/r)&
  1241	 2rI_Ha_P(r)&
  1242	 CI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(1/r)\\[1mm]
  1243	 \dB&
  1244	 2rJ_H(r)&
  1245	 (C/r)J_P(1/r)&
  1246	 CrJ_P(r)&
  1247	 (2/r)J_H(1/r).
  1248	\end{array}
  1249	\]
  1250	The isolated pair values are
  1251	\[
  1252	 a_P^{\Bd}(r)=\frac r{r+1},\quad
  1253	 a_P^{\Bd}(1/r)=\frac1{r+1},\quad
  1254	 a_P^{\dB}(r)=a_P^{\dB}(1/r)=\frac12.
  1255	\]
  1256	
  1257	On macro states the only nonzero rates are
  1258	\[
  1259	\begin{array}{ll}
  1260	 (0,k)\to(1,k):kA,&(0,k)\to(0,k-1):kD,\\
  1261	 (1,k)\to(1,k+1):(q-k)B,&(1,k)\to(0,k):(q-k)C'.
  1262	\end{array}
  1263	\]
  1264	From $(1,k)$, the chance that the center reverses before the next pair
  1265	conversion is $C'/(B+C')$.  A union bound over at most $q$ conversions gives
  1266	an upper bound on failure, because failure requires at least one such
  1267	reversal even though a reversal need not imply failure:
  1268	\[
  1269	 1-P_U^H\le q\frac{C'}{B+C'}.
  1270	\]
  1271	Proposition~\ref{prop:center} and the portal estimates give
  1272	$qC'/B=o(q/C)$, uniformly on each fitness compact.  Thus a mutant center
  1273	converts all resident pairs with probability $1-o(q/C)$.
  1274	
  1275	From $(0,1)$, the first successful macro event is either center invasion at
  1276	rate $A$ or loss of the sole mutant pair at rate $D$.  Hence
  1277	\[
  1278	 P_U^P=\frac{A}{A+D}\,p_{1,1},
  1279	\]
  1280	where $p_{1,1}$ is the macro-fixation probability from $(1,1)$.  The same
  1281	sweep bound, now with at most $q-1$ remaining conversions, gives
  1282	$p_{1,1}\to1$.  (Equivalently,
  1283	$P_U^P=[A/(A+D)][(B+C')/B]P_U^H$.)
  1284	The rate table and the isolated $K_2$ values give
  1285	\[
  1286	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1287	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1288	\]
  1289	More precisely, $A/D\to Z_U$, which yields the stated limits.  The argument
  1290	uses the exact absorbing macro chain, including adverse reversals; no
  1291	branching survival probability is substituted for global fixation.
  1292	\end{proof}
  1293	
  1294	\section{The simultaneous correction and its optimization}
  1295	
  1296	Put $\eta=q/C$.  Substitution of
  1297	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB} and
  1298	Proposition~\ref{prop:gate} into the finite trace gives the key asymptotic
  1299	statement.
  1300	
  1301	\begin{proposition}[Two response functions]
  1302	\label{prop:responses}
  1303	For $C=t^4$, $q=t$, $m/q\to\lambda$, and fixed $\sigma>0$, the separated
  1304	trace satisfies
  1305	\begin{align}
  1306	 \frac{\rho^0_{\Bd,t}(r)}{\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1307	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1308	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)}
  1309	 +\frac{\lambda}{r-1}\right]+o_r(\eta),\label{eq:B}\\
  1310	 \frac{\rho^0_{\dB,t}(r)}{\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1311	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1312	 \frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}
  1313	 -\lambda\right]+o_r(\eta).\label{eq:D}
  1314	\end{align}
  1315	The remainders are uniform on every compact subinterval of $(1,\infty)$.
  1316	\end{proposition}
  1317	
  1318	\begin{proof}
  1319	The complete-graph Bd baseline differs from $p$ exponentially in $C$, while
  1320	the dB baseline differs from $p$ by $O(C^{-1})$; both errors are
  1321	$o(\eta)$.  The center terms follow from
  1322	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}.  The pair terms follow by the
  1323	exact simplifications
  1324	\begin{align}
  1325	 2\left\{\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right\}
  1326	 &=\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\\
  1327	 2\left\{\frac{[1/2]Z_D/(1+Z_D)}p-1\right\}
  1328	 &=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1329	\end{align}
  1330	One leaf replaces one core vertex and contributes
  1331	$1/p-1=1/(r-1)$ under Bd and $-1$ under dB.
  1332	All inputs are compact-uniform by Propositions~\ref{prop:weak-trace},
  1333	\ref{prop:center}, and~\ref{prop:gate}, so the combined remainders are as
  1334	stated.
  1335	\end{proof}
  1336	
  1337	\begin{lemma}[Exact tangency]
  1338	\label{lem:tangency}
  1339	At $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$, both response functions in
  1340	\eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} are strictly positive for every
  1341	$1<r<\Rhyb$ and vanish simultaneously at $r=\Rhyb$.
  1342	\end{lemma}
  1343	
  1344	\begin{proof}
  1345	Both bracketed corrections are positive precisely when
  1346	\[
  1347	 L(r,\sigma)<\lambda<U(r,\sigma),
  1348	\]
  1349	where
  1350	\[
  1351	 L=\frac{2(1-\sigma)(r-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\qquad
  1352	 U=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1353	\]
  1354	After multiplication by positive denominators, $L<U$ is equivalent to
  1355	\[
  1356	 F_r(\sigma)<0,
  1357	\]
  1358	where
  1359	\[
  1360	 F_r(\sigma)
  1361	 =(r-1)\sigma^2+(r^3-4r^2+3r+1)\sigma+r(2r-3).
  1362	\]
  1363	Its minimizing value and minimum are
  1364	\[
  1365	 \sigma(r)=\frac{-r^3+4r^2-3r-1}{2(r-1)},\qquad
  1366	 \min_\sigma F_r(\sigma)=-\frac{P(r)}{4(r-1)}.
  1367	\]
  1368	Exact Sturm counting gives no root of $P$ in $(1,3/2)$ and exactly one in
  1369	$(3/2,151/100)$.  At that root, $\sigma_*$ is the double tangency and
  1370	\[
  1371	 L(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=U(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=\lambda_*.
  1372	\]
  1373	For fixed $\sigma_*$, $L$ is increasing and $U$ decreasing on
  1374	$(1,\Rhyb)$ because
  1375	\[
  1376	 \partial_rL=
  1377	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)\{\sigma(r-1)^2-1\}}
  1378	      {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}^2}>0,\qquad
  1379	 \partial_rU=
  1380	 \frac{4r(\sigma-r)}{\{2r(r-1)+\sigma\}^2}<0.
  1381	\]
  1382	Here $0<\sigma_*<1<r$ and
  1383	$\sigma_*(r-1)^2<1$ throughout the isolating interval, so the displayed
  1384	factorizations determine the signs without numerical approximation.
  1385	Thus
  1386	\[
  1387	 L(r,\sigma_*)<\lambda_*<U(r,\sigma_*)
  1388	 \quad(1<r<\Rhyb),
  1389	\]
  1390	which proves the claim.
  1391	\end{proof}
  1392	
  1393	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
  1394	Fix $r\in(1,\Rhyb)$.  Then $r\in I_t$ for every sufficiently large $t$.
  1395	By Proposition~\ref{prop:responses} and Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}, the two
  1396	separated normalized gains, after multiplication by $n_t/q_t$, converge to
  1397	strictly positive numbers.  The defining inequality for $e_t$ changes each
  1398	scaled gain by at most $1/t$.  Thus both actual connected-graph gains are
  1399	strictly positive for all sufficiently large $t$.  The sequence, including
  1400	every weak-edge weight, was fixed before $r$ was chosen, which is exactly the
  1401	quantifier order in the definition of $\Rsim$.
  1402	\end{proof}
  1403	
  1404	\begin{proposition}[Fixed-parameter response optimality]
  1405	\label{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1406	Among fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute
  1407	pair--pendant response model \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D}, $\Rhyb$ is the exact
  1408	largest threshold of an interval beginning at one on which both corrections
  1409	can remain positive.
  1410	\end{proposition}
  1411	
  1412	\begin{proof}
  1413	For fixed positive $\sigma$, feasibility implies $F_r(\sigma)<0$, and hence
  1414	$\min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)<0$.  At $\Rhyb$ the unconstrained minimum is
  1415	zero at the admissible value $0<\sigma_*<1$.  Exact evaluation gives
  1416	\[
  1417	 P(3/2)=\frac1{64}>0,\qquad
  1418	 P(151/100)=-\frac{39866792399}{10^{12}}<0.
  1419	\]
  1420	The Sturm count in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency} shows that $\Rhyb$ is the only
  1421	root between these endpoints.  Hence $P(r)<0$ on
  1422	$(\Rhyb,151/100]$ and
  1423	$\min_sF_r(s)=-P(r)/\{4(r-1)\}>0$ there.  No positive $\sigma$---indeed no
  1424	real $\sigma$---is feasible immediately beyond $\Rhyb$.  Since the fixed
  1425	pair $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$ works throughout $(1,\Rhyb)$, no interval
  1426	beginning at one can extend farther within this response model.
  1427	\end{proof}
  1428	
  1429	\begin{corollary}[A rational-edge family]
  1430	\label{cor:rational}
  1431	Taking $\sigma=19/137$ and $\lambda=20/27$ gives, exactly at $r=3/2$,
  1432	\[
  1433	 B=\frac{232}{17361}>0,\qquad
  1434	 D=\frac{65}{12123}>0.
  1435	\]
  1436	This choice gives a family with entirely rational edge weights and algebraic
  1437	response threshold
  1438	\[
  1439	 R_{\mathbb Q}
  1440	 =\frac{5069+12\sqrt{147001}}{6439}
  1441	 =1.50176815223369\ldots>3/2.
  1442	\]
  1443	Thus a family with rational internal ratios and rational positive weak edges
  1444	already proves a strict interval beyond $3/2$.
  1445	\end{corollary}
  1446	
  1447	\begin{proof}
  1448	Substitution into \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} gives the two displayed
  1449	margins.  For these rational parameters the dB response first vanishes at
  1450	the unique root in $(1,2)$ of
  1451	\[
  1452	 6439r^2-10138r+703=0,
  1453	\]
  1454	which is $R_{\mathbb Q}$; the Bd response is still strictly positive there.
  1455	Both responses are therefore positive on $(1,R_{\mathbb Q})$ by the same
  1456	monotonicity calculation used in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}.  The weak edges
  1457	remain rational as well: repeat the least-dyadic diagonal construction with
  1458	$I_t=[1+1/t,R_{\mathbb Q}-1/t]$, $\sigma=19/137$, and
  1459	$m_t=\lfloor(20/27)t\rfloor$.  Every edge-weight ratio in this specialized
  1460	family is therefore rational.
  1461	\end{proof}
  1462	
  1463	\section{Discussion}
  1464	
  1465	The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.  One
  1466	weighted graph sequence is chosen without knowing fitness and works for each
  1467	fixed $r$ in an interval extending beyond $3/2$.  A family tuned separately
  1468	at each fitness would establish a weaker statement.  The diagonal choice of
  1469	$\eps_t$ is therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical
  1470	limit: compact convergence selects one positive cut that works
  1471	simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
  1472	
  1473	The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures.  Heavy
  1474	pairs contribute positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub
  1475	pendants contribute positively to Bd and negatively to dB.  Their densities
  1476	are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and the module
  1477	responses appear at the common scale $q_t/C_t$.  The exact Schur trace is
  1478	important here.  It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut
  1479	event and retains the possibility that a nearly successful global sweep
  1480	reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would not
  1481	prove fixation.
  1482	At the singular boundary $\sigma\downarrow0$,
  1483	$F_r(0)=r(2r-3)$ recovers the $3/2$ feasibility endpoint of this response
  1484	model; optimizing over positive $\sigma$ moves the tangency to $\Rhyb$.
  1485	
  1486	Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be
  1487	resolved: reproduction can select a parent before a replacement site, or a
  1488	vacancy can first determine which neighbors compete.  The construction
  1489	shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
  1490	occupy a special environment.  A vanishing fraction of paired niches and
  1491	hub-associated peripheral sites can bias invasion in complementary ways
  1492	while the bulk remains well mixed.  The result is qualitative rather than a
  1493	claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
  1494	structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this
  1495	beneficial-fitness regime.
  1496	
  1497	There are two sharp limitations.  Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1498	optimizes only fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed
  1499	first-order dilute response model.  It does not classify size-dependent or
  1500	singular choices $\sigma_t,\lambda_t$, different pair or pendant density
  1501	scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order boundary, nonseparated
  1502	pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions.  Conversely, the
  1503	paper supplies no finite universal upper bound on $\Rsim$.  Establishing
  1504	such a bound would require a comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on
  1505	arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a classification of this
  1506	construction.
  1507	
  1508	The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small.  Here
  1509	$q_t/C_t=t^{-3}=\Theta(n_t^{-3/4})$, so the normalized fixation gain tends to
  1510	zero.  The pair weights grow like $C_t$, while the exact diagonal may select
  1511	very small positive weak edges.  The proof gives eventual positivity for
  1512	each fixed fitness but no useful finite-size bound on $t_0(r)$.  The floor in
  1513	$m_t=\lfloor\lambda_*t\rfloor$ contributes $O(t^{-1})$ oscillations to the
  1514	scaled finite-$t$ responses, so positivity need not appear monotonically
  1515	before the eventual regime.  The paper makes no claim of empirical or
  1516	finite-population optimality.
  1517	
  1518	\section*{Statements and Declarations}
  1519	
  1520	\noindent\textbf{Data and code availability.}
  1521	No empirical data are used.  The LaTeX source, exact symbolic and rational
  1522	verifiers, labelled-chain audit, and pinned build and replay scripts accompany
  1523	this revision as the deterministic Supplementary Material archive
  1524	\nolinkurl{simultaneous_amplifier_beyond_three_halves_source_and_certificates.tar.gz}.
  1525	It contains an internal SHA-256 manifest and a fresh-environment bootstrap.
  1526	The two pure-Python dependencies are supplied as hash-pinned wheels, so the
  1527	exact certificate replay requires no package-index access once Python 3.14.6
  1528	is available.  Rebuilding the document separately requires externally
  1529	installed Tectonic and Poppler tools and may require Tectonic's standard
  1530	resource cache or endpoint.
  1531	The corresponding development sources are in the
  1532	\href{https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/simultaneous-amplification-beyond-three-halves-v2.0.3/universal_simultaneous_amplification/phase4_landmark_closure/paper_hybrid_threshold}{paper-specific frozen repository directory}.
  1533	The full-repository software snapshot at
  1534	\href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21852072}{doi:10.5281/zenodo.21852072}
  1535	contains the unrefereed v1
  1536	manuscript, source, and software, not this superseding revision.  No new
  1537	persistent identifier has yet been assigned.  When this revision is posted
  1538	as a preprint, the same frozen bundle will be deposited alongside it and this
  1539	statement updated with the assigned identifier.
  1540	The replay enumerates all $512$ configurations and $108$ orbit fibres of its
  1541	finite audit graph and independently checks the response algebra, rational
  1542	margins, Sturm isolation, and tangency.  These certificates audit finite
  1543	transition aggregation and exact algebra; the weak-cut and population
  1544	asymptotics are analytic proofs in the manuscript, not computer-assisted
  1545	consequences of the replay.
