\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal hub-excursion renewal]
\label{lem:reciprocal-renewal}
Uniformly for $r\in\mathcal R$,
\[
 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)=o(C^{-1}),\qquad
 J_H(1/r)=o(C^{-1}).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For fixed $L$, let $X_L$ be the states with resident hub, no mutant pendants,
and at most $L$ mutant ordinary vertices; let $Y_L$ be the analogous states
with mutant hub.  Write $x^U_{C,L}$ and $y^U_{C,L}$ for the largest fixation
probabilities on these two sets.  Lemma~\ref{lem:killed-green} first gives
the coarse bound
\begin{equation}
 x^U_{C,L}\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C}+a_L/C=O_L(C^{-1}).
\label{eq:renewal-coarse}
\end{equation}

We now make the hub-excursion stopping decomposition explicit.  For Bd,
start from $Y_L$.  The hub-loss rate is at least $m$, while the total
ordinary-changing rate is $O_L(1)$ and the pendant-seeding rate is $O(m/C)$.
If hub loss is the first such event, the return state lies in $X_L$ exactly;
charging every other first event as successful fixation gives
\begin{equation}
 y^{\Bd}_{C,L}\leq x^{\Bd}_{C,L}+K_Lm^{-1}+K C^{-1}=o(1).
\label{eq:renewal-y-Bd}
\end{equation}

For dB, let $\zeta$ be the first hub loss.  Until the ordinary count reaches
$\delta c$, the hub-loss intensity is bounded below explicitly:
\[
 q_{h:1\mapsto0}
 =\frac{d-i-\ell}{d+(s-1)(i+\ell)}
 \geq\frac{(1-\delta)c}{d}=:k_h>0.
\]
While the hub is mutant, the ordinary count is dominated by a
subcritical birth--death process with immigration:
\[
 q_i^+\leq b(i+1),\qquad q_i^-\geq d_0i,\qquad b<d_0,
\]
after decreasing $\delta$.  A common-clock monotone coupling gives
$I_s\leq\bar I_s$ for $s\leq\zeta$ until the macro boundary, where $\bar I$ has
birth rate $b(i+1)$ and death rate $d_0i$.  To make its maximum bound
explicit, choose $1<z<d_0/b$ and put $V(i)=z^i$.  Its generator satisfies
\[
 \overline{\mathcal G}V(i)
 =(z-1)z^i\left[b-\left(\frac{d_0}{z}-b\right)i\right]
 \leq K_z-\alpha_zV(i)
\]
for suitable compact-uniform $K_z,\alpha_z>0$.  If
$\bar\tau_M=\inf\{s:\bar I_s\geq M\}$, Dynkin's formula stopped at
$T\wedge\bar\tau_M$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i\!\left(\sup_{s\leq T}\bar I_s\geq M\right)
 \leq (z^L+K_zT)z^{-M}.
\label{eq:immigration-maximum}
\end{equation}
Consequently the ordinary cloud before $\zeta$ is uniformly tight.
Indeed, for fixed $T$ and $L\leq L_1<\delta c$,
\[
 \Prob\!\left(\sup_{s<\zeta}I_s>L_1\right)
 \leq e^{-k_hT}+(z^L+K_zT)z^{-L_1}
 =:\eta_L(L_1,T),
\]
because on the event that the cloud stays below $L_1$ through time $T$, the
hub-loss intensity remains at least $k_h$ throughout.  Thus the first term
is precisely the surviving-hub part of the finite-$T$ split; no
infinite-horizon maximum estimate is being used.
For fixed $T$, let $L_1\to\infty$ and then let $T\to\infty$; the bound
vanishes in that order,
uniformly in $C$ and reciprocal fitness.

On the complementary event, suppress hub reactivation after $\zeta$.
The mutant pendants are then pure deaths, so
$\mathbb E\int_0^\infty\ell_s\,ds\leq m$; the killed-Green bound gives
$\mathbb E\int_0^\tau I_s\,ds\leq a_{L_1}$.  Since the hub-reactivation
intensity is at most $K(I_s+\ell_s)/C$, its probability before pendant
clearance and ordinary extinction is at most
$K(m+a_{L_1})/C$.  Conditional bias also gives
\[
 \Prob_{\{I\leq L_1\}}(\tau_{L_2}<\tau_0)
 \leq K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}.
\]
If neither event occurs, the process enters $X_{L_2}$.  Restoring the
suppressed reactivation only adds its displayed probability, so the strong
Markov property yields
\begin{equation}
 y^{\dB}_{C,L}
 \leq \eta_L(L_1,T)+\frac{K(m+a_{L_1})}{C}
      +K_{L_1}\vartheta^{L_2-L_1}+x^{\dB}_{C,L_2}.
\label{eq:renewal-y-dB}
\end{equation}
For fixed $L_1,L_2,T$, let $C\to\infty$; then let $L_2\to\infty$,
$L_1\to\infty$, and $T\to\infty$ in that order.  Equations
\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} prove
$y^U_{C,L}=o(1)$ for each fixed $L$.

Return now to one ordinary mutant.  At its first hub change, the explicit
hazard-weighted estimates \eqref{eq:green-macro} and
\eqref{eq:green-hub-tail} give a constant $a$ such that
\begin{equation}
 x^U_{C,1}\leq a e^{-\kappa C}
 +\frac aC\bigl(y^U_{C,L}+e^{-\theta L}\bigr).
\label{eq:renewal-refined}
\end{equation}
Therefore
$\limsup_{C\to\infty}Cx^U_{C,1}\leq ae^{-\theta L}$; letting $L\to\infty$
proves $x^U_{C,1}=o(C^{-1})$, while
\eqref{eq:renewal-y-Bd}--\eqref{eq:renewal-y-dB} give
$y^U_{C,0}=o(1)$.  Hence
\begin{align*}
 u_{\mathrm{core}}^{\Bd}(1/r)
 &\leq\frac{C-1}{C}x^{\Bd}_{C,1}+\frac1Cy^{\Bd}_{C,0}
 =o(C^{-1}),\\
 J_H(1/r)
 &\leq x^{\dB}_{C,1}+\frac{y^{\dB}_{C,0}}{c+m}
 =o(C^{-1}),
\end{align*}
because every ordinary clique vertex has degree $c$ and the hub has degree
$c+m$.
\end{proof}

